course: everyday
lesson: 344
title: Putting the blame on someone else
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Blaming someone else is a double wrong: you did the first thing, and then you hurt an innocent person. Archie in Brisbane left the class hamster's cage open, and when the hamster got out he said the new boy did it. Everyone looked at the new boy, who went red and stood alone at playtime for days.
explain: Nobody found out. But Archie felt worse every day he saw the new boy alone. On Saturday he went to confession; the priest listened, gave absolution and a small penance, and Archie felt the knot in his chest loosen. «Now,» said the priest, «the new boy is still waiting.» On Monday Archie told the teacher and said sorry to the boy in front of the class — and they were friends by Friday.
explain: Blame is usually found out by small things: trainer-shaped «dog» footprints, a cat asleep upstairs when the pot fell, a computer history showing the homework was never started, a little sister crying that she didn't eat the cereal. (Her brother put that one right: he said sorry and gave her his share the next morning.)
explain: Blaming also stops you getting better. When Archie blamed the teacher for a bad mark, Mum looked at his rushed book; he had to redo the work, and only when he said «I didn't try» could he ask for the help he needed.
fact: Blaming someone else for your wrong is a double wrong: the wrong itself and the harm to the person blamed.
fact: New children and little children are easy to blame because they can't defend themselves well, which makes it crueller.
fact: Animals and machines can't argue back, so blaming them is still lying.
fact: Computers keep a history of files, so «the computer deleted it» is easy to check.
fact: Blaming others for your bad mark stops you seeing what you need to fix.
fact: Who pays for damage should depend on who did it, not on who can afford it.
fact: In a team, blaming a teammate falsely can get him left out and makes the whole team weaker.
fact: Confession can ease a guilty heart, but the person you blamed still needs your sorry and the truth told.
fact: Clearing the name of the person you blamed, in front of the same people, is part of putting it right.
fact: Owning up quickly stops an innocent person suffering any longer.
exercise: Why did Archie blame the new boy for the hamster? | He was scared, because he had left the cage open | He saw the new boy open the cage door at lunch and walk away | The new boy asked to be blamed | He thought new boys don't mind
exercise: What happened to the new boy while the blame stayed on him? | He was alone at playtime for days | Everyone wanted to be his friend then | Nothing, because nobody believed it | He got a prize for finding the hamster
exercise: After confession, what did the priest say Archie still had to do? | Tell the truth and say sorry to the new boy | Nothing more, it was all forgiven in confession | Light a candle for the hamster | Find a new hamster
exercise: Why is blaming someone else called a double wrong? | You did the wrong and then hurt an innocent person | You must say sorry twice, but both times to the same person | It is twice as funny | It's only one wrong, really
exercise: A boy says the dog made the muddy footprints. How is he found out? | The footprints are shaped like trainers | The dog barks the truth when Mum asks it | Mud tells Mum who it belongs to by its smell | His sister saw him and told Mum straight away
exercise: A boy blames his friend for the broken swing because his family can afford a new one. What is wrong with that? | Who pays should depend on who did it, not on money | Nothing is wrong, because the richer family should always pay | The friend wanted to pay | Swings don't cost anything
exercise: A boy blames his little sister for eating all the cereal. Why is that especially unkind? | Little children can't defend themselves well | Little children like being blamed for things, it's a game | She would have eaten it anyway | Cereal isn't important
exercise: How did that boy put the cereal blame right? | Said sorry to his sister and gave her his share the next morning | Bought more cereal with his pocket money, put it in the cupboard and said nothing | Blamed the dog instead | Told her to stop crying
exercise: Archie blamed the teacher for his bad mark. What did his rushed book show? | That he hadn't really tried | That the teacher marked it wrong | That the book was far too hard | That someone else wrote in it
exercise: Why does blaming others for your own bad mark hurt you too? | It stops you seeing what you need to get better at | It makes the mark go down, because teachers take marks off for blaming | It doesn't hurt you at all | Teachers lower marks for blamers
exercise: A boy tells the coach a teammate missed the pass on purpose. The teammate is left out. What is the fix? | Tell the coach the truth and say sorry to the teammate | Miss a pass on purpose himself in the next match, so it's even | Say nothing, it's done | Tell the team the teammate is bad
exercise: A girl blames the cat for knocking over a plant, but the cat was asleep upstairs. What does this teach? | Animals can't argue back, so blaming them is still lying | Cats often knock plants over anyway, so it's fair to blame the cat | Blaming pets doesn't count | Cats don't mind blame
exercise: A boy says the computer deleted his homework that he never started. How is he caught? | The teacher checks the file history | The computer phones the school | His keyboard shows fingerprints | His friend tells
exercise: Why did Archie say sorry to the new boy in front of the class? | The whole class had heard the blame, so they needed to hear the truth | Teachers make everyone say sorry in front of the class, whatever happened | To show off | Because the boy asked him to
exercise: What is the wise choice when you have made a mess and are scared of being told off? | Own up, even though you're scared | Say a younger child did it while you were out | Say it all happened by itself, somehow | Hide in your room until it blows over
test: Bro, glaring: «Mum shouted at ME for the living room. That was YOU». What should the boy do? | Tell Mum the truth and tidy the room himself | Give the brother a sweet to keep quiet about it | Say the brother is lying | Say they both did it
test: «nobody knows it was me who left the hamster cage open, they all think it's the new kid, sorted». What is true? | The new kid is suffering for his wrong; he should own up | It's fine since the hamster was found safe and well in the end | Sorted, nobody will know | New kids get blamed anyway
test: Mum: «Paw prints with laces? Clever dog.» What is she saying? | She knows the prints are his trainers | She thinks the dog is very clever | The dog wore shoes | She believes his story
test: Friend's dad: «I've paid for that swing. My son swears it wasn't him.» The boy did it. What's right? | Tell the truth so his own parents pay | Stay quiet, his friend's family can afford it | Say they both did it together, half each | Say the swing was old and broke by itself
test: Teacher: «The history shows this file was never opened, Ryan.» What should Ryan say? | «I never did it. Sorry, I'll do it now» | «The computer is lying» | «My brother deleted the history» | «Your computer is broken»
test: Sister, crying: «I DIDN'T eat it!» Mum believes her. The brother did. What fixes it? | Say sorry and give her his share in the morning | Tell her to stop being such a baby about it, it's only cereal | Say Mum ate it | Blame the dog
test: Kid: «I blamed the teacher for my mark and Mum believed me twice, so it works». What's true? | It still stops him learning, and it may not work the third time | It works every time, so he should keep doing it, Mum never checks the books | Teachers are always to blame | Mums never check books
test: Teammate, quiet: «I didn't miss it on purpose, you know». What would put it right? | Tell the coach the truth and say sorry | Say «yeah you did, everyone saw it, mate» | Ignore him | Say the ref was to blame
test: Priest at confession: «And the boy you blamed — does he know the truth yet?» What does Archie need to do? | Tell the teacher and the class it was him | Nothing, the priest knows now | Write the boy an anonymous note | Wait until the boy forgets
test: Mum, sweeping soil: «The cat's been asleep on my bed all morning». What does that tell the girl? | Her blame on the cat didn't work; owning up is best | The cat can sleepwalk and knock plants over in the night | The plant fell by itself | Mum is guessing
test: Which is TRUE about putting the blame on others? | It hurts someone innocent and is usually found out | It's fine if you're very scared of what might happen | It's fine if they're younger | It is never found out
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the attribution of one's own misconduct to another person. | It constitutes a twofold wrong, injuring an innocent party in addition to the original offence | It constitutes no wrong where the other person is younger | It transfers responsibility lawfully to the other person | It is of no consequence where undiscovered
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the attribution of a poor mark to the teacher is disadvantageous to the pupil. | It prevents the pupil from recognising what he must improve | It improves the mark of the pupil, as the teacher then excuses the error | It is required by the rules of the school | It has no effect upon the pupil
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the proper allocation of the cost of damage? | The cost ought to be borne by the person responsible, irrespective of the means of others | The cost ought to be borne by the wealthiest family | The cost ought to be borne by the school in every case | The cost ought to be divided among all who were present
exam: Determine what reparation is required where a pupil has falsely accused a classmate before the whole class. | The truth ought to be stated, and an apology offered, before the same class | A private apology to the teacher suffices, without any disclosure of the truth to the class | No reparation is required following confession | The classmate ought to be compensated with sweets only
